WHAT I delivered:

During FY26, I contributed to the quality and operational readiness of NZ Open Banking through change initiatives and BAU activities. A key BAU responsibility was onboarding and supporting Third-Party Providers and Fourth Parties across non-production and Rehearsal environments. I validated configurations, access, connectivity and Open Banking journeys, investigated onboarding or execution failures, and coordinated their resolution with relevant teams.

I supported the Display Rate migration through test planning and API, integration and regression validation across impacted services. I verified expected behaviour and communicated test evidence and outstanding risks for release decisions.

For Aggregator Configuration and Third-Party Provider repository consolidation, I applied risk-based testing across configuration and MongoDB consistency, onboarding CRUD operations, existing-provider regression, pipeline replay, environment isolation, rollback, recovery and logging. I looked beyond happy-path acceptance criteria to identify defects and scenarios that could affect production stability, data accuracy or onboarding capability.

I validated the cache TTL correction following the dependency behaviour change from seconds to milliseconds, including expected one-hour expiry and consistency across environments. I covered normal, boundary and expiry scenarios to reduce the risk of stale data or premature cache removal. I also supported DR migration verification by checking application and pod health and reviewing evidence through OpenShift, Splunk and Dynatrace.

Across payment and consent API changes, I performed functional, integration, negative and regression testing covering OAuth2/OIDC, authentication and authorisation, mandatory headers, idempotency, error responses, persistence and downstream interactions. I supported partner and Rehearsal testing by investigating issues and coordinating clarification of endpoint, configuration and journey-related questions.

I contributed to the journey-test uplift by strengthening integration and database verification and supporting the approach for moving suitable Postman coverage toward maintainable Playwright end-to-end automation. My focus was to use the appropriate test level, improve regression reliability and enable earlier defect detection through shift-left testing.

HOW I delivered:

I demonstrated ownership by engaging early, clarifying requirements and acceptance criteria, translating technical discussions into testable scenarios, and maintaining visibility of progress, dependencies and blockers. I documented test evidence and residual risks and escalated issues with relevant technical and customer-impact context so stakeholders could make informed decisions.

I collaborated with developers, testers, business analysts, product stakeholders, platform teams and NZ engineering colleagues. For onboarding and Rehearsal activities, I coordinated across teams, followed issues through investigation and provided clear updates. I shared my testing perspective respectfully and constructively challenged assumptions when security, resilience, data integrity or production readiness could be affected.

I balanced planned delivery work with BAU onboarding and time-sensitive partner support, adapting when priorities or technical scope changed. I used evidence from API responses, database validation, application logs and monitoring tools to keep discussions objective. I remained focused on customer and partner outcomes while ensuring delivery speed did not compromise quality or non-financial-risk controls.

I strengthened my capability in Node.js, Jest, Supertest, Playwright and Open Banking standards and applied this learning to test design and technical discussions. Overall, I demonstrated accountability, integrity, collaboration and continuous improvement while supporting reliable Open Banking services and strengthening quality engineering within the team.